{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-12T11:49:51.121Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"72bc6070cdf8e143b3ffadf4","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"argv"},"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.322","source":"probe"}},"op":"create","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/id","value":"pm-3fbj1f"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/title","value":"scanMutationSecrets drops object keys, so an SDK payload such as { api_key: \"<credential>\" } passes every key-based secret rule"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/description","value":"Found 2026-11-12 by the CodeRabbit review of PR 1712 (pm-gh1704). scanMutationSecrets in src/sdk/mutation-guard.ts collects string leaves and scans each value alone; the property name survives only in the reported path. Key-based rules (password_assignment, credential_key_assignment, high_entropy_assignment) need the key and its colon or equals sign in the same string, so a structured SDK or MCP payload whose property is named api_key, client_secret, token or DB_PASSWORD and whose value is a short credential produces no finding. CLI text fields are unaffected because their key appears inside the text. Fix direction: when a leaf's own property name matches a rule's key vocabulary, judge the leaf value with that rule's value grammar and entropy predicate (the structured-spelling data already declares both), reporting the same rule id and path; keep pm's own fields such as token_budget clean."},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/type","value":"Issue"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/status","value":"open"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/priority","value":2},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/tags","value":["mutation-guard","sdk","secret-guard","security"]},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/created_at","value":